Hi [~accountid:6048a13603ceda0069a64c8f] 

It is ok if prolongation period is longer than initial contract.

Only risk I see is already mentioned one - fixed-term contracts are allowed only in specific circumstances described in Labour Law:

* seasonal work;
* work in activity areas where an employment contract is normally not entered into for an unspecified period, taking into account the nature of the respective occupation or the temporary nature of the respective work;
* substitution of an employee who is absent or suspended from work, as well as substitution of an employee whose permanent position has become vacant until the moment a new employee is hired;
* casual work which is normally not performed in the undertaking;
* specified temporary work related to short-term expansion of the scope of work of the undertaking or to an increase in the amount of production;
* emergency work in order to prevent the consequences caused by force majeure, an unexpected event or other exceptional circumstances which adversely affect or may affect the normal course of activities in an undertaking;

If previously we argued that we conclude 3 month contracts based on seasonal work than in case if we prolong it for another year it will be hard to argue such seasonality and we need to think what could be our argument in specific case. 

Worst case scenario - employee goes to Labour Inspectorate or court and claims that his employment contract should be concluded for indefinite period but such claims in practice are very rare.

